.TH FL-SETTINGS 1 "November 2026" "fldesktop 0.1.0" "fldesktop"
.SH NAME
fl-settings \- fldesktop settings
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBfl-settings\fR [\fIPAGE\fR]
.br
\fBfl-settings \-\-list\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
The settings center of fldesktop. Pages are grouped in a sidebar and can be
found with the search field. The search also finds single settings on all
pages (\fBtap\fR finds "Tap to click \(em Mouse & Touchpad"): choosing one opens
its page, scrolls to the setting and makes its label blink. While typing,
the best match is shown: a page whose title has the word, else the best
setting. Most changes apply at once: the compositor
reloads \fIwm.ini\fR, and the panel and desktop restart themselves when their
settings or the theme change. Other applications use a new theme the next
time they start.
.PP
Pages: \fBappearance\fR, \fBlock\fR, \fBprivacy\fR, \fBpanel\fR, \fBlanguage\fR,
\fBdefaultapps\fR, \fBkeyboard\fR, \fBshortcuts\fR, \fBmouse\fR,
\fBdisplays\fR, \fBsound\fR, \fBnetwork\fR, \fBproxy\fR, \fBdatetime\fR,
\fBusers\fR, \fBlogin\fR and \fBabout\fR (Sound and Network are only
present when built with PipeWire and libnm). Other programs whose desktop
entry has \fBCategories=Settings\fR or \fBX\-FLDesktop\-Settings=true\fR are
listed under \fBMore\fR.
.PP
The Displays page uses the wlr-output-management protocol; after a change it
asks whether to keep it and reverts after 15 seconds without an answer.
Language & Region writes \fI~/.config/fldesktop/environment\fR, which
\fBfl-session\fR(1) reads at the next login.
.PP
Appearance offers eight accent colors and a custom one; the fldesktop folder
icons can follow the accent color. Screen Lock sets up \fBfl-lock\fR(1).
Proxy either keeps the proxy variables the session started with or sets
them (no proxy, or manual HTTP, HTTPS, FTP and SOCKS proxies) for the
programs started from then on, through the [environment] section of
\fIwm.ini\fR. Login Screen edits \fI/etc/fldesktop/greeter.ini\fR for
\fBfl-greet\fR(1); saving it runs \fBfl-settings-helper\fR through pkexec,
which asks for an administrator password.
.PP
Privacy turns the list of recently used files on or off and sets how long
it is kept (GTK's \fIsettings.ini\fR for GTK programs, and the text editor),
clears it, and turns the panel's camera and microphone indicator on or off;
it also names the programs that have the camera open.
.PP
Users adds, removes and changes the accounts of people (user IDs 1000 to
59999 with a login shell): full name, account type (an administrator is a
member of \fBwheel\fR, or \fBsudo\fR where there is no wheel group),
password, picture, groups such as \fBlp\fR, \fBuucp\fR or \fBdocker\fR,
and automatic login with greetd (the \fB[initial_session]\fR of
\fI/etc/greetd/config.toml\fR). The changes are made by
\fBfl-users-helper\fR through pkexec with \fBuseradd\fR, \fBusermod\fR,
\fBuserdel\fR, \fBgpasswd\fR and \fBchpasswd\fR; passwords are passed on
its standard input. It refuses to remove your own account, a user who is
logged in, or the last administrator. Pictures are kept in
\fI/var/lib/AccountsService/icons\fR, where \fBfl-lock\fR(1) and
\fBfl-greet\fR(1) find them.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fIPAGE\fR
Open this page, e.g. \fBfl-settings keyboard\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-list\fR
Print the page ids and titles.
.SH FILES
\fI~/.config/fldesktop/wm.ini\fR, \fIdesktop.ini\fR ([Privacy] RememberRecent, RecentDays), \fIpanel.ini\fR,
\fIenvironment\fR, \fI~/.config/mimeapps.list\fR
.SH SEE ALSO
\fBfl-wm\fR(1), \fBfl-panel\fR(1), \fBfl-lock\fR(1), \fBfl-greet\fR(1), \fBfl-mixer\fR(1), \fBfl-network\fR(1),
\fBfl-disks\fR(1)
